{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## FIRST LANDINGS OF THE DRAIN: 2594 and 2607 merged\n\nQueue is draining (18 completed / 9 queued / 3 in-progress). Two changes reached LLM-Review +1 AND\nVerified +1 with no unresolved comments and were submitted:\n\n - 2594 'Split composer EDIT surface into composer_edit' -- MERGED. The last of the three module\n   splits from the janitor epic; 2593 (sizing) and 2596 (acli) landed earlier.\n - 2607 'Schedule childless stories as leaves' -- MERGED. c379's fix.\n\nc379's close gate is running. Evidence posted first, then boxes ticked ONLY (chars unchanged 4776, so\nno stale-material risk), per the lesson that cost two close attempts on S0 earlier.\n\nWHAT 2607 ACTUALLY FIXES, worth restating now that it is on main: next_batch had a DEAD CONJUNCT --\nline 262 already excluded containers structurally, so the story-veto's 'tid not in\nparent_ids_with_children' could never be false and the branch collapsed to 'type == story => never\nschedulable'. A childless EPIC and a childless BUG were both batched; only a childless STORY was not.\nADR 0078 had already decided this exact question -- 'keyed on container vs leaf, NEVER on ticket type'\n-- fixed it in plan-review, and it was never propagated to the scheduler. The branch came in verbatim\nfrom the bash->Python port, not from any decision.\n\nAnd the cost was visible in the tracker before anyone diagnosed it: multiple tickets carried\n'Retyped from story to task after the final scheduling audit: next-batch intentionally skips childless\nstories as planning containers'. Agent commentary INFERRED FROM THE BUG had become the only text\nappearing to defend it. That is the failure mode I later committed myself on a1f1 -- asserting a\nproperty of a gate from observed behaviour instead of reading it.\n\nThe replacement oracle is parameterised over story/task/bug/epic rather than pinning the story case,\nso the same defect cannot return in another type. Under the defect-seeded mutation the [task], [bug]\nand [epic] parameterisations stayed GREEN while [story] failed -- that asymmetry is precisely the\ndefect, and it is now what the test pins.\n\nFive still queued: 2601 (740d), 2609 (S6), 2613 (477b), 2618 (860b -- MAIN'S RED), 2619 (1035)."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788520626190065001,"uuid":"dc44b320-7fc6-44dd-b556-497d7c50e6fc"}